---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pillarByKey } from '../data/pillars';
const pillar = pillarByKey('live')!;
---
<BaseLayout title={`${pillar.label} — Alex Butterfield`} description={pillar.tagline}>
  <section class="phead">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pillar 0{pillar.num}</p>
      <h1>{pillar.label}</h1>
      <p class="lede">{pillar.tagline}</p>
      <p class="muted">
        Before I build anything, I run the live event. This is where the operator instinct comes
        from — reading a room, adjusting on the fly, no do-overs. Every tool in
        <a href="/builder/">Builder</a> traces back to a night like these.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
    <h2>DJ / MC &amp; day-of coordinator — Complete Weddings + Events</h2>
    <p>
      Runs <strong>25–40 live weddings and events a year</strong> — <strong>120+ completed</strong>
      across central Indiana — reading the floor and adjusting song flow in real time rather than
      running a fixed playlist. Often doubles as day-of coordinator: primary point of contact on the
      timeline, orchestrating 2–5 vendors per event so the night stays seamless. Crowds run 100–250
      guests, the largest <strong>1,000</strong>, at venues including The Omni, Ritz Charles, Regions
      Tower, and Laurel Hall.
    </p>
    <ul class="stat-band reveal-group">
      <li class="stat" style="--i:0"><span class="stat__num" data-count="120+">120+</span><span class="stat__label">events completed</span></li>
      <li class="stat" style="--i:1"><span class="stat__num" data-count="90%+">90%+</span><span class="stat__label">Net Promoter Score</span></li>
      <li class="stat" style="--i:2"><span class="stat__num" data-count="1,000">1,000</span><span class="stat__label">largest crowd</span></li>
    </ul>
    <ul class="awards">
      <li>🏆 Rookie of the Year 2023</li>
      <li>🥇 #1 DJ, Q3 2025</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Selected events: Campion (Barn at Bay Horse Inn), Gill (The Wilds), Milholland (Three Fat
      Labs), Long-Merits (Laurel Hall), Kane (Ghibli / Tyler Childers theme), Aping, Lowers
      (alt/rock, no rap, no country), Nortel (The Sycamore at Mallow Run).
    </p>

    <h2>Fight-sport content — BKFC</h2>
    <p>
      Produces fighter interview content and shoot plans under event-day time pressure for BKFC
      athletes Cameron “Jumpman” VanCamp and Damien Black (Indy Boxing and Grappling). Nashville
      media-credential research in progress.
    </p>

    <h2>Olympic weightlifting</h2>
    <p>
      Competes in and films USAW-sanctioned weightlifting meets, working IWF-rule live scoring and
      broadcast logistics. That deep rules familiarity is exactly what made
      <a href="/projects/mind-games/">Mind Games</a> possible — the live experience fed the software.
    </p>

    <h2>Operations at scale — before the events</h2>
    <p>
      The live-event instinct didn’t come from nowhere. It was built on years of high-throughput
      operations where the same rules apply — volume, deadlines, and a team that has to stay in sync.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Amazon (DIN1 + Return Center)</strong> — coordinated same-day delivery and returns
        across teams of <strong>25–50</strong>, moving up to <strong>~25,000 packages a day</strong>,
        and trained new associates on the logistics and scanner systems.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quickship Brands</strong> — ran a returns-and-repair line under
        <strong>R2 V3 e-waste compliance</strong>: disposition decisions, electronics diagnostics
        and repair, secure data erasure, and fraud verification on eBay return claims.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Mill Top</strong> — coordinated up to <strong>3 simultaneous events</strong>
        (100–250 guests each) and executed full room flips between them in <strong>under 15
        minutes</strong>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      This is the operations spine behind the <a href="/next-chapter/">AGM chapter</a> — the same
      execute-under-pressure profile, applied to logistics and teams rather than a dance floor.
    </p>

    <p class="next"><a href="/builder/">Next: how the events became software →</a></p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .phead { padding-block: 3rem 1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .prose { padding-block: 2rem 3rem; }
  .awards {
    list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .6rem;
  }
  .awards li {
    background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px; padding: .35em .9em; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--accent-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .next { margin-top: 2rem; font-weight: 600; }
</style>
